\description{
Computes quantities of a whole community, draw by draw, from a model
of many series. A community is every series at one site and time.
Each gets its expected number of species, its diversity and its
community-weighted mean traits. Each species gets its probability of
presence. Each pair of species gets how often it occurs together
beyond what the covariates predict. Pass \code{newdata} as a covariate
gradient, such as one from \code{\link[=datagrid]{datagrid()}}, to see how the community
changes along it.
}
\details{
\emph{Richness}. Each draw sums the species' probabilities of presence,
giving the expected number present. A count family's probability is
one less its probability of a zero, which covers zero inflation and
hurdles. Under \code{\link[=occ]{occ()}} it is the occupancy probability and under
\code{\link[=nmix]{nmix()}} the probability that the latent abundance exceeds
\code{threshold}. Richness counts the species the model holds.

\emph{Latent state}. Species share their latent factors or trends, which
move them together. Because presence is a non-linear function of the
latent state, a typical site differs from an average one. Under
\code{latent = "marginal"} each draw averages presence over \code{n_nodes}
latent states drawn from the trend's stationary covariance across
series. The draws of the latent state are shared by every community,
which makes a difference between communities free of their noise. A
\code{RW()} or \code{LLT()} trend has no stationary covariance. Each draw then
averages over the latent states it fitted at the training times.

Under \code{latent = "forecast"} each draw propagates its last fitted state
\code{n_nodes} times with fresh innovations, as \code{\link[=forecast_uncertainty]{forecast_uncertainty()}}
does. Presence is averaged over these paths. The communities need
times after the training data. Communities at one time share the
paths. A pair's excess co-occurrence then comes from the uncertainty
in the forecast state, which grows with the horizon.

\emph{Diversity}. A family with an abundance, such as a count or a
biomass, weights each species by its expected value. Under \code{\link[=nmix]{nmix()}}
the weight is the latent abundance. Hill numbers of order one and two
(the exponential of Shannon entropy and inverse Simpson) follow from
the relative weights. A binary or occupancy family has no abundance.
Its weight is the probability of presence, which gives the expected
trait of the species present. Hill numbers are then left out. Each
measure is averaged over the latent states, giving the expected value
at one site. Under a count family the weights confound abundance
with detection.

A factor trait gives one column per level, whose weighted mean is the
share of that level.

\emph{Co-occurrence}. Species sharing a latent state appear together more
or less often than their covariates alone predict. A pair's excess
co-occurrence is the probability that both are present less the
product of their probabilities, averaged over the communities. A
pair the latent state moves independently has none. Neither does any
pair under \code{latent = "conditional"} or \code{"zero"}, which fix the state.
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
# Detections of 6 species at 80 sites, 4 visits each. Occupancy
# responds to the site covariate `env` and detection to the time of
# day `tod_c`. Two latent factors drive the species associations.
simdat <- sim_closure_unit_data(
  family    = occ(),
  n_species = 6L,
  n_sites   = 80L,
  n_visits  = 4L,
  n_lv      = 2L,
  seed      = 1L
)

# A joint occupancy model. Each species has its own occupancy
# intercept and `env` slope, plus its own detection intercept. Two
# latent factors take up the residual association.
mod <- jsdgam(
  formula        = bf(y ~ series * env, p ~ series + tod_c),
  factor_formula = ~ -1,
  data           = simdat$data_train,
  family         = occ(),
  n_lv           = 2L,
  chains         = 2,
  silent         = 2
)

summary(mod)

# The effect of `env` on the chance of a detection, occupancy times
# detection
conditional_effects(mod)

# The residual correlations among species the factors imply
plot(residual_cor(mod))

# Site scores and species loadings on the two factors
ordinate(mod, rotation = "varimax")

# Expected richness along `env`. The second plot marks the pairs of
# species found together more often than `env` predicts.
gradient <- datagrid(model = mod, env = seq(-2, 2, length.out = 20))
cm <- community(mod, newdata = gradient)
plot(cm)
plot(cm, type = "cooccurrence")

# The draws, for any contrast: richness at the end of the gradient
# less that at its start
rv <- as_draws_rvars(cm)
rv$richness[20] - rv$richness[1]

# Next, compare n_lv = 1 with update(mod, n_lv = 1L) and loo()
}

}
